Taliesin is built around one idea: writing technical content should feel as
immediate as editing code. You save the file, and only the block you changed
re-renders. Your scroll position, your live canvas, and your warm kernel all stay
exactly as they were. No cold start, no full-page flash, no waiting.

{{< include _includes/three-scene.tmd >}}

```{js}
//| echo: false
const makeScene3D = tali.get("makeScene3D");
const reduce = matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
const host = document.createElement("div");
host.style.minHeight = "440px";
const build = async () => {
  let el;
  try {
    el = await makeScene3D((scene, THREE) => {
      const n = 56;
      const geo = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(6, 6, n, n);
      const p = geo.attributes.position;
      for (let i = 0; i < p.count; i++) {
        const x = p.getX(i), y = p.getY(i), r = Math.hypot(x, y);
        p.setZ(i, 0.7 * Math.sin(r * 2) * Math.cos(x * 0.9));
      }
      geo.computeVertexNormals();
      const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geo, new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
        color: 0x3a5578, metalness: 0.12, roughness: 0.5, side: THREE.DoubleSide,
      }));
      mesh.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2.3;
      scene.add(mesh);
      const key = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.1);
      key.position.set(4, 6, 5);
      scene.add(key);
    }, invalidation, {
      width: 720, height: 440, cameraPos: [0, 5.5, 7.5],
      ambientIntensity: 0.9, autoRotate: !reduce, minDistance: 4, maxDistance: 14,
    });
  } catch (e) {
    el = document.createElement("p");
    el.className = "tali-muted";
    el.textContent = "The 3D preview needs network access to load Three.js from esm.sh.";
  }
  host.replaceChildren(el);
};
if (reduce) build();
else {
  const io = new IntersectionObserver((es, o) => {
    if (es[0].isIntersecting) { o.disconnect(); build(); }
  });
  io.observe(host);
  invalidation.then(() => io.disconnect());
}
return host;
```

This scene is live WebGL. Drag to orbit it. It is the `{js}` cell above plus a shared
`makeScene3D` helper that `{{< include >}}` pulls in from `_includes/three-scene.tmd`,
which loads Three.js from esm.sh, so the scene needs network access. More of them, and
the source, on [the showcase](showcase.tmd).

```sh
taliesin preview post.tmd   # http://127.0.0.1:4321, updating as you save
```

## One source, three shapes

Write once in plain text, and the shape follows the structure: one file is a post, a
directory with a `_site.yml` is a website, and a `chapters:` list makes it a book. All
three render to HTML and share the same block model, the offline math and highlighting,
and the live edit loop, so anything you learn for one carries to the rest.

::: {.feature-list}
::: {.feature}
### Blog posts & papers
Prose, math, figures, citations, and cross-references, with executable Python
cells and live interactive graphics.

[See the gallery →](https://gallery.taliesin.sh/)
:::

::: {.feature}
### Multi-chapter books
A sidebar, numbered chapters, cross-chapter references, and full-text search. These
very docs are a Taliesin book.

[Read the docs book →](https://guide.taliesin.sh/)
:::

::: {.feature}
### Multi-page websites
Navbar, footer, OpenGraph, Cmd-K search, and opt-in post listings with an Atom feed per
dated listing. This whole site is a Taliesin website, built by the same `build` you run.

[Recipes for books and sites →](https://guide.taliesin.sh/using/recipes.html)
:::
:::

## Edit the code. The output re-runs in place.

A `{python}` cell runs against a warm kernel. Change a parameter and only
that cell (and the figure it produced) re-renders. The rest of the page never
moves, and the kernel never cold-starts.

@fig-live is not a screenshot of that: it is the output of a cell in this page's own
source, executed by the same `build` that wrote the HTML you are reading.

```{python}
#| label: fig-live
#| fig-cap: "Drawn by a Python cell in this page's source as the site was built. Change the number of cycles, save, and only this figure re-renders."
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

cycles = 6
t = np.linspace(0, 1, 600)
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 3))
ax.plot(t, np.sin(2 * np.pi * cycles * t) * np.exp(-2.2 * t), lw=2)
ax.set_xlabel("t")
ax.set_ylabel("amplitude")
plt.show()
```

## Three more things worth knowing

::: {.feature-list}
::: {.feature}
### Click to source
Ctrl-click (Cmd-click on Mac) anything in the preview and your editor jumps to the line
it came from, even across files pulled in with `{{< include >}}`. It is the one bridge
back: the `.tmd` stays the only place you edit.
:::

::: {.feature}
### Bundled, not fetched
Fonts, KaTeX and its font files, syntax highlighting and the vendored plotting libraries
are all compiled into the binary and written into the page. Taliesin itself never fetches
anything at runtime, so a built page renders the same offline. Anything *you* `import()`
from a `{js}` cell is your own network call, which is why the scene above says so when it
cannot reach esm.sh.
:::

::: {.feature}
### Output you can host anywhere
One command turns a document into a single self-contained HTML file, or a portable folder
with its local assets copied alongside. Point it at a directory instead and you get a
whole site or book as static files, with no server runtime and nothing to reproduce.
:::
:::

This page, the surface you spun at the top of it, and the
[documentation book](https://guide.taliesin.sh/) it links to are all built and served by
Taliesin, from plain `.tmd` files in one repository.
